## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cross-store consistency, idempotent writes, conflict handling, and disaster recovery across polyglot persistence in a real-time collaboration application.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent multi-store writes across Redis, MongoDB, Cassandra, DynamoDB
- Cross-region conflict handling with eventual consistency
- Replay/restore path and tombstone semantics for recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: handle save edit across stores with client_write_id
async function saveEdit(doc, delta, clientWriteId) {
  // Redis: cache latest version atomically
  await redis.hSet(`doc:${doc.id}`, {
    content: doc.content,
    version: doc.version,
    clientWriteId
  });

  // MongoDB: upsert with revision tracking
  await db.documents.updateOne(
    { id: doc.id },
    { 
      $set: { 
        content: doc.content,
        version: doc.version,
        updatedAt: new Date(),
        clientWriteId
      }
    },
    { upsert: true }
  );

  // Cassandra: append immutable delta
  await cassandra.execute(
    'INSERT INTO document_edits (doc_id, rev, ts, user, delta, client_write_id) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?, ?)',
    [doc.id, doc.version, Date.now(), doc.userId, delta, clientWriteId]
  );

  // DynamoDB: update daily aggregates
  await dynamodb.update({
    TableName: 'daily_aggregates',
    Key: { doc_id: doc.id, date: new Date().toISOString().split('T')[0] },
    UpdateExpression: 'ADD edit_count :inc SET last_edit = :ts',
    ExpressionAttributeValues: { ':inc': 1, ':ts': Date.now() }
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to craft end-to-end cross-region data flows with idempotence, dedup, and TTL across Redis, DynamoDB, MongoDB, Cassandra. Evaluates data movement, consistency guarantees, and failure handling in distributed systems.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent writes across stores
- De-duplication using composite keys
- TTL handling in Redis and DynamoDB
- Change data capture across regions (streams)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: dedupe and propagate fraud signal
async function handleSignal(region, signalId, userId) {
  const dedupKey = `${region}:${signalId}`;
  if (await redis.get(dedupKey)) return;
  await redis.set(dedupKey, '1', { EX: 60 * 60 * 24 });
  // DynamoDB put with conditional to avoid duplicates
  await dynamo.putItem({ id: `${region}#${signalId}`, userId, ts: Date.now() }, { ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(id)' });
  await cassandra.log({ region, signalId, userId, ts: Date.now() });
  await mongodb.updateOne({ userId }, { $set: { lastFraudAt: new Date() } });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test fault injection across regions?
- How would you handle late-arriving signals that predate an existing alert?

## Why This Is Asked
